#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:1
msgid "`frontend/gda_expr` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:3
msgid "`gda_expr` parses General Decimal Arithmetic `.decTest` text and executes the resulting cases against the independent `decimal_gda` backend. The production package does not import the IEEE `decimal` engine."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:7
msgid "Parsing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:9
msgid "`parse_dectest(source_name, text)` returns `GdaDocument` or an array of `ParseDiagnostic`. Documents expose `source`, `cases`, and `case_count`. `GdaCase` exposes ID, operation, normalized operation, operands, expected text, conditions, context, expression, and source span. `GdaContext` exposes precision, rounding, exponent bounds, clamp, extended mode, and dectest mode."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:15
msgid "Execution"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:17
msgid "`execute_documents(documents, options?)` returns `RunSummary`. `RunOptions::new` configures shard count/index, strict-supported behavior, and a case filter. Summaries expose total, selected, executable, passed, failed, skipped, diagnostic, legacy, and unsupported counts plus individual results; `merge` combines shard summaries and `success` reports gate status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:23
msgid "`CaseDisposition` distinguishes executable rows from `#` placeholder diagnostics and reserves legacy/unsupported states for future corpus changes. `CaseResult` exposes ID, pass state, disposition, and message. The pinned legal GDA corpus has no unsupported or legacy rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/gda_expr.md:28
msgid "Exact accessors are `GdaDocument::{source,cases,case_count}`; `GdaCase::{id,operation,normalized_operation,operands,expected,conditions,context,expression,span}`; `GdaContext::{default,precision,rounding,min_exponent,max_exponent,clamp,extended,dectest}`; `RunOptions::{new,shard_count,shard_index,strict_supported,case_filter}`; `RunSummary::{total_cases,selected_cases,executable_cases,passed_cases,failed_cases,skipped_cases,diagnostic_cases,legacy_cases,unsupported_cases,results,success,merge}`; and `CaseResult::{id,passed,disposition,message}`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/itl_expr.md:1
msgid "`frontend/itl_expr` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/itl_expr.md:3
msgid "`parse_itl(text)` returns typed `ItlCase` values or diagnostics. `execute_case(case, precision?)` returns an `ItlResult`; `summarize_results` folds results into counts and retains individual rows. Callers inspect `ItlDisposition` to distinguish executable, unsupported, and diagnostic cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/itl_expr.md:8
msgid "The package accepts only the operations and expected-value forms implemented by the current parser/executor. Consult `testdata/interval/README.md` for the strict conformance subset."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:1
msgid "`frontend/mpfr_expr` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:3
msgid "`parse_sqrt_data`/`execute_sqrt_data` handle MPFR hexadecimal sqrt rows. `parse_pow_data`/`execute_pow_data` handle the pinned integer-power witness format. `parse_elementary_data`/`execute_elementary_data` handle the pinned 29-operation matrix. Documents expose source and case count; summaries expose total, passed, failed, individual results, and `success`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/mpfr_expr.md:9
msgid "Only these three repository-pinned grammars are accepted. Diagnostics preserve source, line, and message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:1
msgid "`frontend/testfloat_expr` API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:3
msgid "`TestFloatSpec::parse(function, rounding, tininess?)` validates format, operation, rounding, and tininess. `parse_testfloat(source, text, spec)` returns a typed document; `execute_document(document, options?)` executes stable shards and returns result/count accessors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend/testfloat_expr.md:8
msgid "Supported operations are `Add`, `Subtract`, `Multiply`, `Divide`, and `SquareRoot` over binary16/32/64/128. A successful summary means selected rows matched values and flags; it does not expand the supported matrix."
msgstr ""

