msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:82
msgid "Elementary functions use directed dyadic certificates over `BinCoeff`. Each series or reduction step rounds outward, and an explicit analytic remainder bound encloses the truncated tail. The implementation uses:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:86
msgid "range reduction plus a bounded exponential series for `exp`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:87
msgid "power-of-two reduction plus a certified unit-interval series for `ln`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:88
msgid "certified logarithms and exact shortcuts for `log2`/`log10`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:89
msgid "Machin-formula bounds for pi, certified quadrant reduction, alternating series, and exact critical-point tests for `sin`/`cos`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:91
msgid "the same reduction plus pole detection for `tan`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:92
msgid "monotonic endpoint evaluation and domain splitting for inverse/hyperbolic functions; and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:94
msgid "`exp(exponent × ln(base))` with guarded outward arithmetic for general positive-base power."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:97
msgid "The shared certification budget permits 12 refinements and increases work precision by `max(32, work / 2)`. `try_*` operations expose range-reduction, resource-limit, or target-rounding failures as structured certification errors. Total interval operations use conservative set fallbacks when their contract permits it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:103
msgid "uncertified or very large `sin`/`cos` returns `[-1, 1]`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:104
msgid "a `tan` pole or uncertified reduction returns Entire;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:105
msgid "analogous composed operations choose their documented whole-domain bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:107
msgid "This boundary is intentional. The `try_*` family is appropriate when proof failure must be observable; the total family is appropriate when a valid but possibly wide enclosure is more useful than stopping a calculation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:111
msgid "Critical Points, Poles, And Switching Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:113
msgid "Endpoint evaluation alone is sufficient only for monotone functions. Periodic functions additionally compute a certified range of quadrant/critical indices. If that range contains an extremum, the corresponding `-1` or `1` endpoint is included. If it contains a tangent pole, the result is Entire. The decision is made in exact/certified index space, not by comparing an approximate remainder to a heuristic epsilon."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:120
msgid "For bounded ordinary arguments, refinement attempts a tight certificate. For unbounded arguments or arguments whose adjusted binary exponent exceeds the resource cutoff `max(65,536, 4 × precision)`, the total path switches directly to the safe range fallback; the `try_*` path returns a resource-limit detail. This is a proof-resource boundary, not a claim that the mathematical function is undefined."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:127
msgid "Decorations And Relations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:129
msgid "Decorations propagate by the weakest operand/operation grade. Domain clipping or discontinuity can lower the grade even when the bare enclosure is valid. Set observers retain their interval meaning:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:133
msgid "`contains` asks whether a point belongs to the set;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:134
msgid "`subset`, `interior`, and `set_equal` compare sets;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:135
msgid "`overlap_state` classifies relative geometry;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:136
msgid "`definitely_lt`/`definitely_gt` prove an order only when all points satisfy it;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:137
msgid "`maybe_eq` reports compatibility rather than equality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:139
msgid "No total scalar order is defined for intervals. Likewise, `Sign::Zero` may mean that the set crosses zero rather than that it is the singleton `{0}`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:142
msgid "Optimization And Trade-offs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:144
msgid "Basic interval operations perform a constant number of endpoint operations, so their cost is `O(M(p))` at endpoint precision `p`, with storage for two `BinFloat` values. Elementary functions add range reduction, bounded series, and up to 12 refinement steps. Their cost is data-dependent because difficult rounding cells and large arguments require more proof precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:150
msgid "The implementation optimizes repeated constants, exact special cases, monotonicity, and shared trigonometric reduction before increasing precision. It does not optimize by dropping an endpoint candidate or replacing outward rounding with nearest rounding. When tightness and resource bounds conflict, the total API widens and the checked API reports why."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:156
#: manual/design/bin_float.md:156
#: manual/design/decimal.md:176
#: manual/design/decimal_gda.md:169
msgid "0.8.0 Semantic Preservation Proof"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:158
msgid "For each finite endpoint candidate `y`, downward rounding is a lower certificate and upward rounding is an upper certificate. The interval code first selects the mathematical extremum from monotonicity and sign structure, then applies the matching direction; `quantize_interval` preserves the same outward relation at the stored precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:164
msgid "The integer-power proof obligations are explicit: positive odd powers increase, positive even powers decrease on the negative half-axis, negative odd powers decrease away from zero, and negative even powers increase on the negative half-axis. Zero-containing negative powers use the pole/whole-real branches. The negative-half-axis regression, directed endpoint tests, and strict ITF1788 aggregate verify ordered set inclusion for the declared forward interval surface. A wider fallback is valid; a reversed or inward-rounded endpoint is not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/ball_float.md:173
