#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:1
msgid "Numeric Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:3
msgid "This page defines the vocabulary shared across the `0.8.0` documentation. It distinguishes mathematical value from stored representation, rounding status, checked failure, and interval enclosure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:7
msgid "Value And Representation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:9
msgid "A finite binary value is a signed non-negative coefficient multiplied by a power of two. A finite decimal value is a signed non-negative coefficient multiplied by a power of ten. Different representations can denote the same mathematical value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:14
msgid "For decimal values, `12.3400`, `12.340`, and `12.34` are one numeric value but different cohorts. Their stored exponent is the quantum. Parsing preserves that quantum; `normalized()` or `reduce_ctx()` may remove trailing coefficient zeros and adjust the exponent without changing the mathematical value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:19
msgid "Precision is representation metadata and a rounding bound, not a proof that every stored digit is significant. `with_precision` may round. It does not change the radix or convert between binary and decimal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:23
msgid "Rounding And Contexts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:25
msgid "The shared binary-style rounding vocabulary contains nearest-even, toward-zero, toward-positive, toward-negative, and away-from-zero. Decimal adds GDA modes such as half-up, half-down, and zero-five-up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:29
msgid "`BinaryContext`, `DecimalContext`, and `BallContext` make bounded-format policy explicit. Depending on the domain, a context carries precision, exponent bounds, clamp, rounding, and tininess detection. Presets such as `binary64`, `decimal64`, and the corresponding ball context supply standard bounds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:34
msgid "Use a context API when any of the following is observable:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:36
msgid "exact rounding direction;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:37
msgid "overflow, underflow, subnormal, inexact, or rounded status;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:38
msgid "before- versus after-rounding tininess;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:39
msgid "decimal clamp or exponent cohort rules;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:40
msgid "a fixed interchange format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:42
msgid "Flags, Status, Traps, And Errors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:44
msgid "An operation flag reports a condition raised while producing a defined result. Flags are not automatically exceptions. Combine explicit IEEE flags across steps when accumulated status is required."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:48
msgid "GDA distinguishes three related values:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:50
msgid "`raised`: conditions produced by the current operation;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:51
msgid "`status`: sticky union of conditions from all threaded operations;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:52
msgid "`traps`: enabled conditions that change `Completed` into `Trapped`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:54
msgid "A trapped `GdaOutcome` still contains the GDA-defined result, next context, and raised flags. Trap selection follows a fixed precedence when multiple enabled signals are raised."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:58
msgid "`ArithmeticError` is different: it describes a checked operation that cannot produce the requested scalar result under the checked capability contract. Binary and interval result wrappers short-circuit that error. Decimal composition instead preserves its native state: `DecimalChecked` accumulates IEEE flags around defined results, while `GdaDecimalChecked` threads sticky status and short-circuits traps without erasing their defined results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:65
msgid "Special Scalar Values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:67
msgid "Binary and decimal scalars expose signed zero, positive/negative infinity, quiet NaN, and signaling NaN where supported. NaN payload and signaling state are representation data; arithmetic may quiet a signaling NaN and raise an invalid condition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:72
msgid "Numeric equality and ordering do not imply representation equality:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:74
msgid "signed zeros compare numerically equal but retain different signs;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:75
msgid "decimal cohort members compare numerically equal but may fail `same_quantum`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:76
msgid "NaN is unordered under ordinary comparison;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:77
msgid "total comparison operations deliberately order representations, including NaNs and cohorts, for sorting and protocol use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:80
msgid "Use classification and explicit total-order APIs instead of inferring behavior from formatted text."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:83
msgid "Interval Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:85
msgid "`BallFloat` denotes a set of reals bounded by binary endpoints. Arithmetic must contain every mathematical result of choosing one value from each input set. Outward rounding protects this inclusion invariant."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:89
msgid "The important states are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:91
msgid "**Empty**: no real value;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:92
msgid "**Entire**: all real values;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:93
msgid "**non-empty bounded or unbounded interval**: values between endpoints;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:94
msgid "**NaI**: decorated invalid interval state, distinct from Empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:96
msgid "Containment, subset, overlap, disjointness, and definite comparison replace scalar total ordering. Division by an interval containing zero can validly produce Entire. A conservative enclosure is a successful value even when it is not tight."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:101
msgid "Decorations record how strongly the result satisfies continuity/domain conditions. They are not scalar flags and are not stored by `BallFloatResult`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:105
msgid "Conversion And Projection"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:107
msgid "Binary-to-decimal and decimal-to-binary conversion may require rounding because finite values in one radix are not always finite in the other. Choose precision and rounding explicitly when the conversion result is part of a contract."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:111
msgid "Interchange conversion is narrower than arbitrary-precision conversion: binary16/32/64/128 and decimal32/64/128 enforce fixed field widths, exponent bounds, special encodings, and status behavior."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:115
msgid "`semantic` projection is exact for the mathematical value it retains, but it is intentionally lossy for representation metadata. Use it for cross-package comparison and diagnostics, never to round-trip quantum, payloads, signed zero, decorations, or flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:120
msgid "Decision Checklist"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:122
msgid "Before choosing an API, answer:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:124
msgid "Is the required result a scalar value, a representation, or a real set?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:125
msgid "Must radix, precision, exponent bounds, or quantum remain observable?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:126
msgid "Does the caller need per-operation flags, sticky GDA status, or checked short-circuit errors?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:128
msgid "Can NaN, infinity, signed zero, Empty, Entire, or NaI occur?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:129
msgid "Is ordinary partial comparison sufficient, or is total order/set relation required?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:131
msgid "Is conversion arbitrary precision or fixed interchange?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/numeric_semantics.md:132
msgid "Which finite conformance matrix supports the claimed behavior?"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:1
msgid "`ball_float` Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:5
#: manual/performance/decimal.md:5
#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:5
msgid "Measurement Boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:7
msgid "`just bench ball-float --target native` runs the current Maremark suite in `src/bench/ball_float` and writes `.tmp/bench/ball-float.jsonl` plus its analysis file. The artifact measures layer cost on the current target; it is not an IEEE 1788 completeness claim or a release-wide latency guarantee."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:12
#: manual/performance/decimal.md:13
#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:14
msgid "Workload"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:14
msgid "The suite measures add, multiply, and divide at 53, 128, 512, and 2,048 bits. Each cell compares the binary kernel, core `BallFloat`, and full checked path. The expected value is an exact binary result, and the ball and checked outputs must match it as singleton enclosures before timing observations are accepted."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:19
#: manual/performance/decimal.md:20
#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:21
#: manual/tutorial/bench.md:10
#: manual/tutorial/bench/ball_float.md:9
#: manual/tutorial/bench/bin_float.md:11
#: manual/tutorial/bench/decimal.md:9
#: manual/tutorial/bench/decimal_gda.md:9
msgid "Reading Results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:21
msgid "`MAREMARK_JSONL` is the raw event stream and `MAREMARK_HOTSPOT` reports paired kernel-to-core and core-to-checked overhead. A lower layer cost does not imply a tighter enclosure: directed rounding, decorations, poles, and conservative fallbacks remain semantic responsibilities of the core path."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:33
msgid "Compare artifacts only with the same target, toolchain, workload, and benchmark protocol. Normal benchmark tests compile plans but skip timing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:36
#: manual/performance/decimal.md:38
#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:41
msgid "Semantic Gate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/ball_float.md:38
msgid "Performance evidence is subordinate to enclosure correctness. The 0.7.1 interval gate passes 4,656/4,656 selected strict ITF1788 cases, including the integer-power regression boundary. See [Conformance](../conformance/ball_float.md), [Design](../design/ball_float.md), and the [0.7.1 performance and semantic audit](../performance_audit.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:1
msgid "`bin_float` Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:6
msgid "Contract"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:8
msgid "Performance thresholds, limb layout, NTT primes, scratch storage, and fallback selection are implementation details. Public results, flags, and interchange bits must be identical across algorithms and targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:12
msgid "Non-JS targets use inline 64/128-bit coefficients and little-endian 32-bit limbs; JavaScript uses a hidden host `bigint` adapter. The public `BinCoeff` type keeps that backend choice unobservable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:14
msgid "Multiplication"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:16
msgid "Balanced multiplication uses schoolbook below 96 limbs, then Karatsuba, Toom-3, and a two-prime Montgomery NTT with CRT when transform bounds fit. Unbalanced inputs use block multiplication or overlap-add. Native/LLVM currently select Toom-3 and NTT multiplication at 2,048 limbs and NTT square at 768; Wasm/Wasm-GC use 4,096 and 3,072. Native square dispatch switches from specialized schoolbook to recursive multiplication at 512 limbs; other targets retain the previous 768-limb boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:18
msgid "Division, Square Root, And GCD"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:20
msgid "Division uses a one-limb path, Knuth below 48 divisor limbs, Burnikel–Ziegler from 48, and Newton reciprocal division from 1,024. Square root uses fixed-width kernels through 512 bits and divide-and-conquer above that. Large GCD uses Lehmer batching."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:22
#: manual/performance_audit.md:79
msgid "Measurement"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:24
msgid "The unified Maremark harness under `bench/bin_float` compares coefficient kernels, numeric cores, checked full paths, and semantically equivalent square candidates with balanced blocks. Auto-tune emits a versioned per-scale policy and applies a threshold only to the measured target; every threshold change still requires correctness tests on all targets."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:26
msgid "The elementary release gate separately archives the immutable `0.6.1` commit and the dirty candidate tree, injects one identical add/mul/div/sqrt workload, and collects ten alternating AB/BA native pairs at 53, 128, and 512 bits. Maremark blocks release only when the candidate is at least 3% slower and the 95% bootstrap interval has a positive lower bound. Functions first introduced in `0.7.1` run as a candidate workload; this release becomes their first valid baseline, because inventing `0.6.1` timings for APIs that did not exist would be meaningless."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:35
msgid "Trade-offs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/bin_float.md:37
msgid "Schoolbook minimizes setup and allocation, recursive algorithms reduce asymptotic work, and NTT improves very large balanced inputs at the cost of transforms and temporary storage. Every fast path retains an exact fallback, so performance dispatch cannot alter semantics."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:1
msgid "`decimal` Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:7
msgid "`just bench decimal --target native` runs the current Maremark suite in `src/bench/decimal` and writes `.tmp/bench/decimal.jsonl` plus its analysis file. This is a reproducible measurement artifact, not an immutable release gate. There is no checked-in decimal performance manifest or threshold workflow in 0.7.1; current instructions use the Maremark suite below."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:15
msgid "The suite measures add, multiply, and divide at 9, 34, 128, and 512 decimal digits. Each cell compares the coefficient kernel, the core `Decimal` path, and the full checked path against an exact `BigInt` reference. Input creation and expected-value construction stay outside the timed payload."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:22
msgid "`MAREMARK_JSONL` is the raw versioned event stream and `MAREMARK_HOTSPOT` is the paired layer-overhead analysis. Results describe this tree, toolchain, and target. They are useful for finding a hotspot, but they do not establish a universal crossover or a release-wide latency bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:34
msgid "The all-suite run also covers binary, GDA, and interval kernels. It is safe to compare artifacts only when the target, toolchain, workload, and benchmark protocol are held constant."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal.md:40
msgid "Benchmark equivalence checks are necessary but not sufficient. Optimized decimal routes must also pass coefficient differential tests and the pinned IEEE decimal and GDA conformance gates before they are accepted. See [Conformance](../conformance/decimal.md), [Design](../design/decimal.md), and the [0.7.1 performance and semantic audit](../performance_audit.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:1
msgid "`decimal_gda` Performance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:7
msgid "`just bench decimal-gda --target native` runs the current Maremark suite in `src/bench/decimal_gda` and writes `.tmp/bench/decimal-gda.jsonl` plus its analysis file. The independent quick fixture can be run with `python3 tools/run_gda_benchmark.py`; it writes `.tmp/bench/gda-quick-native.json` and uses three native runs per cell. Neither artifact is a universal speed claim or a substitute for the conformance gate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:16
msgid "The Maremark suite measures add, subtract, multiply, divide, FMA, and parse at 1, 9, 18, 34, and 128 decimal digits. It compares the core `GdaContext` path with the full checked path and validates both against the core result before reporting layer overhead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:23
msgid "`MAREMARK_JSONL` is the raw event stream and `MAREMARK_HOTSPOT` reports paired core-versus-checked overhead. The quick fixture additionally records per- operation medians and dispersion. Interpret these measurements only for the recorded target, toolchain, fixture, and workload; they do not establish a cross-target threshold or a fixed latency bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:37
msgid "Use the same target and toolchain when comparing artifacts. Normal benchmark tests compile their plans but skip timing, so an explicit benchmark command is required for measurement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/performance/decimal_gda.md:43
msgid "The timing path is accepted only alongside the independent GDA state model and the pinned legal scalar corpus: 64,986/64,986 current rows and 16,124/16,124 legacy rows pass in the 0.7.1 audit. See [Conformance](../conformance/decimal_gda.md), [Design](../design/decimal_gda.md), and the [0.7.1 performance and semantic audit](../performance_audit.md)."
msgstr ""

