#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:19
msgid "Parse Without Losing Quantum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:21
msgid "Parsing preserves the input exponent when the coefficient fits the requested precision. Trailing zeros can therefore carry application meaning."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:36
msgid "`normalized()` changes the cohort but not the mathematical value. Do not call it automatically when scale is part of a monetary, measurement, or protocol record."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:40
msgid "Use `parse` when invalid text needs an `ArithmeticError`; use `from_string` when `None` is sufficient."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:43
msgid "Calculate Exact Decimal Values"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:45
msgid "Ordinary operators are convenient for unconstrained calculations whose working precision is already encoded in the operands."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:58
msgid "For externally specified precision, rounding, or exponent bounds, use context operations instead of relying on operand precision."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:61
msgid "Use A Context And Preserve Flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:63
msgid "`DecimalContext` is immutable. Each `*_ctx` operation returns a value and flags raised by that operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:82
msgid "Keep combined flags next to the value for the lifetime of the calculation. `has_error()` is a convenience policy, but applications should inspect individual signals when inexact, rounded, subnormal, overflow, or clamped behavior matters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:87
msgid "Quantize Deliberately"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:89
msgid "Use `quantize` when the output exponent is part of the contract. The target quantum is supplied as a `Decimal` value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:100
msgid "`quantize` reports invalid-operation when the requested exponent cannot fit the context. It does not silently choose another scale."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:103
msgid "Encode Decimal Interchange"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:105
msgid "Select both format and encoding explicitly when exchanging bits with another system."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:124
msgid "Use `BID` only when the external protocol requires it. GDA concrete interchange lives in `decimal_gda` and is DPD-only; do not move encoded values between the packages by assuming their contexts are interchangeable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:130
msgid "Use `try_*_ctx` at a boundary where certification failure must remain observable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:148
msgid "The implementation converts the exact decimal to directed dyadic bounds and accepts a result only when both bounds round to the same decimal value and flags. Convenience methods such as `log10_ctx` use the same proof path but treat proof failure as unrecoverable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:153
msgid "Convert Between Decimal And Binary Carefully"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:155
msgid "Dyadic values convert exactly to decimal when enough decimal precision is available. Most finite decimal fractions do not convert exactly to binary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:176
msgid "When building an interval around a decimal real value, convert twice with `TowardNegative` and `TowardPositive` and use both bounds. A nearest-rounded binary point embedded with `BallFloat::exact` encloses only that binary point, not necessarily the original decimal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:181
msgid "Special Values And Ordering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:183
msgid "Signed zero, infinities, quiet/signaling NaNs, and payloads are explicit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:184
msgid "`compare` is numerical and is not a total representation order over NaNs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:185
msgid "`compare_total` orders representations, including cohorts and NaNs, for deterministic storage/protocol use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:187
msgid "`same_quantum` tests exponent/cohort compatibility, not numerical equality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:188
msgid "Classification under a context (`is_normal`/`is_subnormal`) may differ from a context-free finite/special classification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:193
msgid "Preserve parsed quantum until the application has explicitly chosen to normalize or quantize."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:195
msgid "Use one context through a calculation and combine every returned flag."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:196
msgid "Treat interchange encoding as an IO boundary; do arithmetic on `Decimal`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:197
msgid "Use `try_*_ctx` for elementary functions on untrusted inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:198
msgid "Use `decimal_checked` for a closed IEEE pipeline; use `decimal_gda` or `decimal_gda_checked` for sticky GDA status and traps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:203
msgid "[Design](../design/decimal.md) explains coefficient dispatch, finalization, and certified decimal rounding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:205
msgid "[Conformance](../conformance/decimal.md) defines the IEEE evidence boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:206
msgid "[Performance](../performance/decimal.md) records target-specific crossover policy."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal.md:207
msgid "[`decimal_checked` tutorial](decimal_checked.md) shows automatic flag accumulation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:1
msgid "`decimal_checked` Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:3
msgid "`DecimalChecked` is a closed IEEE decimal pipeline. It stores the current defined `Decimal`, one IEEE `DecimalContext`, flags raised by the latest step, flags accumulated across all steps, and an optional certification error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:7
msgid "Accumulate IEEE Flags"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:27
msgid "`raised()` describes only the last operation. `flags()` is the combined history. `outcome()` returns the current value with the combined flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:30
msgid "Keep Defined Exceptional Results"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:32
msgid "IEEE conditions usually retain a numerical result:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:50
msgid "`is_ok()` means that no `ArithmeticError` stopped the pipeline. It does not mean that IEEE flags are empty. Certification failures from elementary `try_*` operations enter `error()` and short-circuit later steps; rounded, inexact, overflow, or division-by-zero conditions remain value-plus-flags outcomes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:55
msgid "Reset A Status Window"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:57
msgid "`clear_flags()` keeps the current value and context but clears both latest and accumulated flags. Use it after an application has recorded one calculation phase."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:61
msgid "`with_context(new_context)` reapplies the current value under the new IEEE context and records any resulting flags. It is an observable calculation step, not a metadata-only setter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:65
msgid "Do Not Merge Independent Pipelines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:67
msgid "Binary methods accept a plain `Decimal`. This avoids inventing a rule for merging two contexts and two flag histories."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:75
msgid "If two independent checked calculations must be combined, first decide at the application boundary which context and flag-merging policy is correct."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:80
msgid "Inspect `raised()` for step-local policy and `flags()` for end-to-end policy."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:81
msgid "Treat `is_ok()` and `flags().has_error()` as different questions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:82
msgid "Clear flags only after the previous status window has been consumed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:83
msgid "Use `result()` at the outer boundary when certification failure must be returned to the caller."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:85
msgid "Use `decimal_gda_checked` instead when sticky GDA status and traps are required."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_checked.md:88
msgid "See [Design](../design/decimal_checked.md) for the transition model and [`decimal` tutorial](decimal.md) for raw context operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:1
msgid "`decimal_gda` Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:3
msgid "Use `decimal_gda` when a calculation must follow General Decimal Arithmetic rounding, sticky status, defined trap results, and trap precedence. Use [`decimal`](decimal.md) instead for the IEEE `(value, flags)` model, DPD/BID interchange, or the broader 0.8.0 elementary-function surface."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:8
msgid "The One Rule To Remember"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:10
msgid "Every operation returns a `GdaOutcome`. Thread `next_context()` into the next operation when status must accumulate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:17
msgid "`raised()` describes the current operation. `next_context().status()` is sticky and describes the full threaded calculation so far."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:20
msgid "Parse And Calculate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:34
msgid "The parse result preserves the input cohort. Division receives the context returned by parsing, so any parse condition remains in the sticky status."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:37
msgid "Read Raised And Sticky Status"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:52
msgid "Use `clear_status()` to start a new observation window while retaining traps. Use `reset()` only when both status and trap configuration should return to defaults."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:56
msgid "Configure And Handle A Trap"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:58
msgid "Trap sets are immutable. Enabling a trap creates a new context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:80
msgid "The defined infinity remains available. The trap changes control flow; it does not replace the GDA result with a generic error. When several enabled signals are raised, inspect `trapped_signal()`/the `Trapped` case rather than inventing an application-side precedence order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:85
msgid "Validate Dynamic Contexts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:87
msgid "Use `try_new` for configuration or user input:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:98
msgid "This keeps invalid precision or reversed exponent bounds in the normal error channel instead of aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:101
msgid "Quantize And Preserve Cohorts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:103
msgid "Use GDA `quantize` when the result exponent is prescribed:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:113
msgid "Thread `outcome.next_context()` after observing Rounded/Inexact or an invalid quantize request. Call `reduce` only when canonical cohort form is intended."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:116
msgid "Use Mathematical Functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:118
msgid "The GDA surface intentionally exposes only the standard-facing mathematical family implemented by the package: square root, power, `exp`, `ln`, and `log10`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:129
msgid "Trigonometric, hyperbolic, inverse, `atan2`, `hypot`, and pi-scaled operations belong to the IEEE decimal/binary and interval surfaces, not to this GDA adapter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:132
msgid "Choose The Right Comparison"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:134
msgid "`compare` performs quiet numerical comparison and returns a decimal comparison value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:136
msgid "`compare_signal` applies signaling comparison behavior."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:137
msgid "`compare_total` orders complete representations, including NaNs and cohorts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:138
msgid "`compare_total_magnitude` applies total order to magnitudes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:139
msgid "`same_quantum` tests cohort exponent compatibility."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:141
msgid "Use total comparison for deterministic sorting or protocol canonicalization; do not substitute it for ordinary numerical equality."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:144
msgid "Use The Checked Pipeline For Long Chains"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:146
msgid "Manual context threading is clearest at integration boundaries. For a long linear pipeline, `decimal_gda_checked` retains one `GdaOutcome`, threads sticky status, and stops automatically after a trap:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:162
msgid "Use `resume_defined()` only after the application has explicitly decided that continuing from a trapped operation's defined result is valid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:165
msgid "Common Mistakes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:167
msgid "Reusing the original context when sticky status should accumulate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:168
msgid "Treating `Trapped` as “no value.”"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:169
msgid "Combining `GdaFlags` manually and assuming a context status changed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:170
msgid "Mixing `decimal.Decimal` and `decimal_gda.Decimal` as if they were aliases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:171
msgid "Using `decimal_checked` for a GDA pipeline; it deliberately implements the different IEEE flag model."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:173
msgid "Depending on coefficient thresholds or cache behavior; neither is public API."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:177
msgid "Construct or validate one context at the calculation boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:178
msgid "Thread every returned `next_context()` in manual workflows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:179
msgid "Inspect both current `raised` flags and sticky `status` at control boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:180
msgid "Match `Trapped` explicitly and record the defined result before deciding to resume or stop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:182
msgid "Use `decimal_gda_checked` for linear composition, but return to raw outcomes where branching trap policy is application-specific."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:187
msgid "[Design](../design/decimal_gda.md) explains the state transition, kernel isolation, and switching boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:189
msgid "[API reference](../api/decimal_gda.md) lists the complete legal scalar surface."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:190
msgid "[Conformance](../conformance/decimal_gda.md) defines the pinned GDA corpus claim."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda.md:191
msgid "[`decimal_gda_checked` tutorial](decimal_gda_checked.md) covers trap short-circuit and recovery in detail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:1
msgid "`decimal_gda_checked` Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:3
msgid "`GdaDecimalChecked` stores exactly one GDA outcome and threads its sticky context through a linear calculation. It is the recommended composition layer when a trap should stop later operations until recovery is explicit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:7
msgid "Thread Sticky Status"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:23
msgid "`raised()` describes the latest operation. `status()` comes from the next context and includes all previously threaded conditions. `outcome()` exposes the complete `Completed` or `Trapped` state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:27
msgid "Stop At A Trap"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:46
msgid "The value remains the GDA-defined infinity. Later operations are identity transitions while the outcome is trapped, so no calculation accidentally runs past the configured control boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:50
msgid "Resume Only By Policy"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:68
msgid "`resume_defined()` retains the defined value and sticky context, clears the current-step raised observation, and returns a completed pipeline. Call it only after the application has logged/handled the trap and decided that continuing from the defined result is valid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:73
msgid "Keep Binary Operands Plain"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:75
msgid "Methods such as `add`, `multiply`, and `quantize` accept a plain GDA `Decimal`. This prevents implicit merging of two independent sticky contexts. If two pipelines meet, select the controlling context and status policy outside the wrapper."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:82
msgid "Check `is_trapped()` or `trapped_signal()` at business control boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:83
msgid "Record `raised()`, `status()`, and the defined value before recovery."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:84
msgid "Never use `resume_defined()` merely to make a pipeline continue."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:85
msgid "Use raw `decimal_gda` outcomes when trap handling branches into several application actions; use this wrapper for linear composition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/decimal_gda_checked.md:88
msgid "See [Design](../design/decimal_gda_checked.md) and the raw [`decimal_gda` tutorial](decimal_gda.md)."
msgstr ""

